+// SourbyCraft - Canvas's base/ Minecraft patches USE several ca.spottedleaf.concurrentutil.*
+// classes (SchedulableTick, etc.) without any patch actually creating/modifying those files, so
+// the generic importCanvasLibraryFiles patch-scan (which only auto-imports a class when some
+// patch's diff contains a matching "+++ b/<path>" line) never vendors them as editable source —
+// they stay resolved from the plain "leafpile" library dependency, whose access modifiers don't
+// match what canvas.at widens, so canvas-server code that reads e.g. SchedulableTick.state fails
+// to compile ("is not public ... cannot be accessed from outside package"). Point the task at our
+// own explicit import list (root build-data/canvas-dev-imports.txt) instead of the upstream
+// checkout's empty one. See that file for the full rationale.
+// afterEvaluate: the fork-setup wiring (setupFork -> CoreTasks, applied by the
+// dev.iyanz.sourbypatcher.core plugin) configures importCanvasLibraryFiles's devImports itself
+// from project.afterEvaluate, which runs AFTER plain top-level script statements — a top-level
+// .configure{}/configureEach{} here would be clobbered right back to the upstream checkout's
+// (empty) dev-imports.txt. Registering our own afterEvaluate later in script order runs after the
+// plugin's, so it applies last and wins.
+afterEvaluate {
+    tasks.withType<io.papermc.paperweight.core.tasks.ImportLibraryFiles>().matching { it.name == "importCanvasLibraryFiles" }.configureEach {
+        devImports.set(rootProject.layout.projectDirectory.file("build-data/canvas-dev-imports.txt"))
+    }
+}
